Date: Fri, 10 Sep 93 00:35:36 -0400
From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: BOOK: C.J.Cherryh, anyone?

Phil writes:

  I'm on a mailing list for the discussion of Cherryh's works;
  I've seen at least one other extropian there, and was wondering
  if any of you liked her work.

Prepare for ridiculous fan-boy-like gushing (embarrassing but unstoppable).

Like it?! I just spent two days devouring _Hellburner_ and _Chanur's Legacy_!  
I had to physically restrain myself from stealing them from the bedside of my  
fiancee, Kelly. Cherryh has to be my favorite SF author (Kelly's too). I feel  
that her "Merchanters' Universe" cycle is as great an accomplishment as  
Niven's "Known Space". And I had vowed at 18 never to love space opera again!

It seems to me that Cherryh has an almost painful knowledge of the depths of  
human (and trans-human) loneliness and despair, and their roots in  
misunderstanding and fear. But at the same time, she recognizes the  
transcendent ability we have to overcome *any* barrier - emotional, physical,  
or cultural. Her stories inspire me and make me hungry to get *out* there!

Her human characters are more real to me than those of any other writer. And  
her aliens are amazing. She has the knack of creating creatures and cultures  
that are *completely* foreign to any I know of and at the same time,  
completely convincing and tenable. Some of them, for example the Kif of the  
Chanur series, have had profound effects on the way I view human behaviour.

There are several meta-themes that seem to reoccur in her SF work that I  
think would be very appealing to extropian readers;

 - the idea that while large organizations (states, corporations, cultures)
   may seek to contain and limit the scope of human affairs, they can never
   completely succeed. As self-aware creatures, we are incapable of being
   ultimately defined. We will continue to change, evolve, expand in ways
   which we are ourselves often incapable of forseeing.

 - the idea that fear and ignorance are the basis of most human suffering
   and "evil" (and I use that word with caution, as I doubt that Cherryh
   believes that such a thing as "evil" exists). Communication and knowledge
   are the ultimate tools of a conscious mind.

 - the realization that it is individuals who are ultimately responsible
   for the shape which the universe takes and the course of human affairs.
   We are each of us at the helm of destiny.

OK, Ok, I'll be quiet now.

But really, if you haven't read her stuff, check out the Chanur series (pub'd  
by DAW, 1st book _The Pride of Chaunur_). The fact that the only *human*  
person in the book is not the main character is forgotten by the time you get  
a couple chapters in. You won't be sorry.

P&J,
 Jeff

p.s. Phil, would you forward me the edress of that mailing list? Thanks.


