Subject: Can backwaters like Louisiana make it in the coming era?

Tim May:
>Low wage rates are not everything. Technological infrastructure and
>the overall investment climate plays a big role, as does the local
>work ethic. 

I'd be interested in a more in-depth discussion about what 
constitutes "technological infrastructure".  What aspects of
it are physical and fixed, and what aspects are mobile?
Given increased bandwidth and more sophisticated use of
"cyberspace" in the next couple decades, which pieces of
"infrastructure" will become mobile?  For example, the
on-line library right now is just in a primitive phase --
primarily on-line catalogs and a few famous books with
expired copyrights.  Copyright seems to stand in the way
of the on-line bookstore.  So I'm still dependent on
the local bookstores and university libraries. That's one
reason I'm on an extended visit to the Bay Area right now
The other reason my interest in meeting with a 
tiny minority of the people down here.  Alas, these Californians 
have quite fascist physical neighbors.

As for social gatherings, I find this list as stimulating as
face-to-face get-togethers, albeit the two complement each
other.  To e-mail and news I'd like to add a "virtual whiteboard" 
in both e-mail and real-time talk modes.  This can be had with 
some experimental X-windows programs right now, but I'd like to see 
versions using SLIP or even more primitive protocols running on Mac 
and Windows.  I also hope the cost of travel goes down still further (or
my travel budget goes up :-) so I can attend more physical
get-togethers like Extropaganza, cypherpunks mettings, GP
lunches, Assembler Multitudes, etc. without having to put up with the 
fascist neighbors.

>Of
>course, in the telecommuting age, only certain folks will be in
>demand--the clue-challenged masses will not be in much demand compared
>to their harder-working, better-educated counterparts in other regions
>of the world. I just don't see any scenario in which there are enough
>high-paying jobs for most people--but this is another debate.

There will be many skilled physical jobs, from housing
construction to massage, for a long time to come.   These
workers will be physically fixed but highly paid.  Information
workers can become more mobile if the many subtle problems of
tele-info-capitalism -- copyright, privacy, independence of
network address from physical location, etc. -- can be
solved.  The main problem in employing people, as always,
will be retraining them to figure out and tackle new markets.
Human needs and desires for improved entertainment, medicine,
etc. are insatiable; there is always a shortage of people
who can meet those needs.

