Date:     Fri, 10 Sep 93 10:37:21 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  The Discoveries of Concepts and Rights

Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu> describes a scenario in which
human rights are not compossible.  This is irrelevant to the question of
whether they exist.

Having generally given up on trying to teach those extropians afflicted
with the methodological monism I call "engineeritis," I won't bother arguing
with most false claims about the moral concept denoting and affirming
man's freedom of action in a social context, a.k.a., "rights."  However,
the following goes too far:

>You can draw a kind of parallel with concepts. Concepts are a human
>invention, that is, the invention of intelligent, rational creatures.
>They are not "objective" only in the sense that we did not discover
>them, but invented them: similarly, we did not discover rights, but we
>invented them.

Not only are concepts most certainly not any kind of invention whatsoever,
neither are rights.  To dispose of the claim that concepts are made, rather
than found, requires nothing more than to consider the question: how can
anyone invent anything without already having any concepts?  They can't.
This is the fallacy of self-exclusion, also known as the problem of self-
referential refutation.  (Most of the worst fallacies of today are of this
kind.)

Both concepts and rights are most certainly discoveries, not inventions.

The fundamental error in the discussion of rights is the conceptual dualism
stemming from Aristotle (in which case he actually did make a mistake, unlike
his metaphysics) that all things are either made by man, or not a result of
human action at all.  This omits spontaneous orders altogether.  (It continues
to astound me how so many who post to this list fail to appreciate fully the
significance and implications of this concept!)

Primarily, rights are not the result of human design, just like money,
language, and many other social formations.  They result from human action,
but not from human design.

The notion that these things are made, rather than found, is just as
creationist as the notion that the Universe and everything in it are the
Creation of some Supreme Being.  There is no such Supreme Being, Divine
or secular.

