Tim, what is your definition of "concept"?[1] It sounds like you and
Ratha might be talking about different levels of abstraction here.

I conceive the minimal concept of a "concept" (since we're dicing and
slicing self-references anyway) is a remembered image _with
associations_. The second dictionary definition[1] "concept = thought"
is not very useful, since we don't know how to observe thoughts in
others.

At this rather low level of abstraction, we have yet to part company
with at least the higher primates, since they seem to keep track of
the mental state of themselves and their fellows, which is hard to
explain by modeling a concept-less mind.

(In case it's not clear, I'm agreeing with your proof, for all
definitions of "concept" and "invention" with which I am comfortable.)

|> Both concepts and rights are most certainly discoveries, not inventions.

What have you said above that entitles you to throw "rights" back
into the mix? The utility of rights does seem like a "discovery", if
we can use that term for "stable product of cultural evolution"; but
certainly by the time humans began to _argue_ about rights, they were
directing the future evolution of them.


|> The fundamental error in the discussion of rights is the conceptual dualism
|> stemming from Aristotle (in which case he actually did make a mistake, unlike
|> his metaphysics) that all things are either made by man, or not a result of
|> human action at all.  This omits spontaneous orders altogether.  (It continues
|> to astound me how so many who post to this list fail to appreciate fully the
|> significance and implications of this concept!)

No one on this list has, to my knowledge, "failed to appreciate"
spontaneous orders. Hell, it's in our charter! Even the recent
defenders of "strong" Natural Rights derived them ultimately from
evolutionary considerations.

|> Primarily, rights are not the result of human design, just like money,
|> language, and many other social formations.  They result from human action,
|> but not from human design.

What justifies dropping money in with language and other
culturally-evolved pragmas? They are all of prehistoric, indeed
probably preliterate, provenance, but if you know of a definitive
history or well-supported theory of the development of money that
shows that Ook didn't have to *explain* to Og that Ook's four cowrie
shells could be exchanged for one of Og's spearheads because Mogh had
gone fishing that morning and would almost certainly trade a fish for
two cowries[2] when he got back ... it's a few levels of abstraction
higher and, unlike language, has no clear precedent in our instinctual
heritage. Courtship gifts by penguins and other species don't qualify
because they don't circulate and they don't have multiple uses.

|> 
|> The notion that these things are made, rather than found, is just as
|> creationist as the notion that the Universe and everything in it are the
|> Creation of some Supreme Being.  There is no such Supreme Being, Divine
|> or secular.

We observe a nested series of abstractions, and when the stack gets
high enough and the agent smart enough, they can be applied in novel
ways by will, in other words, design happens. I don't know how to
construct a universal test for the difference.

